UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 7, Lesson 14: Playbuilding', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"A story beat is one action that pushes the key scene forward. This lesson adds sequence to the audience-knowledge list from last time. Three beats are enough: find, obstacle, decision. Here, find means discovering the key is missing, not getting it back. The obstacle is the problem that blocks an easy fix. The office shutter is down, so a replacement key is unavailable. The decision is the choice made because of that obstacle: Riley will search the art room, where the bag was at lunch. Order matters. The find creates the need, the obstacle removes the easy path, and the decision answers it. Write the three beats in that order. Performing them is optional, with consent and an adult nearby.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same arts idea to a new artwork or performance, then compare two choices for clarity of effect.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nRiley has an empty pocket and the office shutter is down. Which order of beats is find, then obstacle, then decision?\n\nFEEDBACK\nDiscover the loss, meet the closed office, then choose the art-room search. The empty pocket is the find. The shutter is the obstacle. Turning toward the art room is the decision. Choosing the art room first would decide before the audience knows what is wrong. Finding the key in beat one would also skip the obstacle, and this lesson stops before the key returns.\n\nWATCH FOR\nDeciding where to go before any obstacle leaves nothing to decide. A decision needs a reason. The closed office is that reason. Another mix-up is treating find as ''the key is back in the hand''. In this sequence, find means the loss is discovered. Put the beats back in order: loss, block, choice.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nWrite find, obstacle, and decision beats for a missing sports bib. The bib is not in the gym bag, the spare tub is locked, and the student must choose a next place to look. Label the three beats in order. Do not use the house key or the art room. The writing is the evidence. Acting the beats is optional, with consent and an adult nearby.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one artistic choice you changed and why. Movement or instrument work is optional, adult-supervised and can be described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Drama","learning_intention":"A story beat is one action that pushes the key scene forward. This lesson adds sequence to the audience-knowledge list from last time. Three beats are enough: find, obstacle, decision. Here, find means discovering the key is missing, not getting it back. The obstacle is the problem that blocks an easy fix. The office shutter is down, so a replacement key is unavailable. The decision is the choice made because of that obstacle: Riley will search the art room, where the bag was at lunch. Order matters. The find creates the need, the obstacle removes the easy path, and the decision answers it. Write the three beats in that order. Performing them is optional, with consent and an adult nearby.","lesson_number":14,"lesson_role":"Apply","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: scrap paper, crayons, safe classroom instruments or clear movement space with an adult nearby"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["DR4-MAK-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Write find, obstacle, and decision beats for a missing sports bib. The bib is not in the gym bag, the spare tub is locked, and the student must choose a next place to look. Label the three beats in order. Do not use the  Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using story beat on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: Deciding where to go before any obstacle leaves nothing to decide.. Correction: Discover the loss, meet the closed office, then choose the art-room search.. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Deciding where to go before any obstacle leaves nothing to decide. A decision needs a reason. The closed office is that reason. Another mix-up is treating find as ''the key is back in the hand''. In this sequence, find means the loss is discovered. Put the beats back in order: loss, block, choice. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating story beat on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with story beat, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about story beat with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for story beat. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for story beat. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Deciding where to go before any obstacle leaves nothing to decide","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named","Copy the example artwork or script and call that independent work","Discover the loss, meet the closed office, then choose the art-room search"],"question":"Riley has an empty pocket and the office shutter is down. Which order of beats is find, then obstacle, then decision?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for story beat; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Careful use of obstacle with clear evidence","Deciding where to go before any obstacle leaves nothing to decide","A story beat is one action that pushes the key scene forward","Discover the loss, meet the closed office, then choose the art-room search"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with story beat?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named obstacle, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Deciding where to go before any obstacle leaves nothing to decide","Name obstacle only, without linking it to story beat","Apply story beat to new material and name the obstacle used","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence"],"question":"Which response best applies story beat with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The stronger choice uses elements, techniques and audience effect appropriately for this lesson on story beat. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Discover the loss, meet the closed office, then choose the art-room search","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named","Copy another artwork’s sacred design without permission or guidance","Deciding where to go before any obstacle leaves nothing to decide"],"question":"Which response best evaluates elements, techniques and audience effect when analysing story beat?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Deciding where to go before any obstacle leaves nothing to decide","Name a specific element or technique and say what effect it creates for story beat","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named","Copy another artwork’s sacred design without permission or guidance"],"question":"Which evidence move best supports a claim about story beat?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea (A story beat is one action that pushes the key scene forward.) with concrete detail. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Say it looks nice with no element or technique named","Copy another artwork’s sacred design without permission or guidance","The beats are the empty pocket, the closed office, and the choice to search","Deciding where to go before any obstacle leaves nothing to decide"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about story beat?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Accuracy means checking evidence and honouring limits or protocols. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Treat every claim as equally reliable without checking a source or cue","Name the artistic choice, its effect, and keep cultural protocols respectful","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete","Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster"],"question":"Which action keeps elements, techniques and audience effect accurate for story beat?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the taught skill. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Retype the worked example with no new example","Deciding where to go before any obstacle leaves nothing to decide","Name obstacle only, with no linked example","Write find, obstacle, and decision beats for a missing sports bib"],"question":"Which independent response best applies story beat to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about story beat with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating story beat on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using story beat on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for story beat. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with story beat, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: Deciding where to go before any obstacle leaves nothing to decide.. Correction: Discover the loss, meet the closed office, then choose the art-room search.. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent artwork or performance notes and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s5-drama-y9-w11-l2","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 4 Drama 7-10 (2023) alignment to DR4-MAK-01. Content-point mapping pending. Not NESA verified.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S4","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise story beat.","Read the mission before you start making or performing.","Keep the audience effect of your choice in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not name an effect clearly."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the element or technique that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore elements, safety or respectful practice.","Prefer a named artistic reason over a guess that merely looks colourful."],"submit":["Confirm that your choice and reason are both present.","Proofread element names and safety notes.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact element, technique or cue before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key artistic choices.","Say what each choice does for the artwork or audience.","A final praise word alone is weaker than a named effect."],"teach2":["Compare a weak choice with a stronger one.","Change the element or technique, not only the decoration.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh subject or piece that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original work notes and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill on new material."]},"subject":"Drama","success_criteria":["I can use story beat with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify obstacle and explain why it matters.","I can practise decision independently on a new prompt.","I can notice sequence needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"A story beat is one action that pushes the key scene forward.","options":["A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no artistic reason","A story beat is one action that pushes the key scene forward"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about story beat?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nThe beats are the empty pocket, the closed office, and the choice to search. Find: Riley pats the zip pocket and shows Alex an empty hand. Obstacle: Alex points at the office shutter, which is already down. Decision: Riley says the bag was in the art room at lunch and turns that way. A swapped order shows why sequence matters. If they choose the art room before anyone has seen the empty pocket, the audience has no problem yet. The key itself is still not found. That recovery belongs to a later lesson.\n\nTRY FIRST\nRiley has an empty pocket and the office shutter is down. Which order of beats is find, then obstacle, then decision?\n\nFEEDBACK\nDiscover the loss, meet the closed office, then choose the art-room search. The empty pocket is the find. The shutter is the obstacle. Turning toward the art room is the decision. Choosing the art room first would decide before the audience knows what is wrong. Finding the key in beat one would also skip the obstacle, and this lesson stops before the key returns.","explain":"A story beat is one action that pushes the key scene forward. This lesson adds sequence to the audience-knowledge list from last time. Three beats are enough: find, obstacle, decision. Here, find means discovering the key is missing, not getting it back. The obstacle is the problem that blocks an easy fix. The office shutter is down, so a replacement key is unavailable. The decision is the choice made because of that obstacle: Riley will search the art room, where the bag was at lunch. Order matters. The find creates the need, the obstacle removes the easy path, and the decision answers it. Write the three beats in that order. Performing them is optional, with consent and an adult nearby.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Deciding where to go before any obstacle leaves nothing to decide. A decision needs a reason. The closed office is that reason. Another mix-up is treating find as ''the key is back in the hand''. In this sequence, find means the loss is discovered. Put the beats back in order: loss, block, choice. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"The find beat of an empty pocket beside the obstacle of a closed office shutter.","caption":"Find the loss first, then meet the obstacle.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Find</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">Empty pocket at the gate</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Obstacle</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">Office shutter is down</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this example: The beats are the empty pocket, the closed office, and the choice to search. Find: Riley pats the zip pocket and shows A Then write your own artistic reason.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.\nJuno: Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this example: The beats are the empty pocket, the closed office, and the choice to search. Find: Riley pats the zip pocket and shows A Then write your own artistic reason."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no artistic reason","Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about story beat?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nThe beats are the empty pocket, the closed office, and the choice to search. Find: Riley pats the zip pocket and shows Alex an empty hand. Obstacle: Alex points at the office shutter, which is already down. Decision: Riley says the bag was in the art room at lunch and turns that way. A swapped order shows why sequence matters. If they choose the art room before anyone has seen the empty pocket, the audience has no problem yet. The key itself is still not found. That recovery belongs to a later lesson. Look at each line, sound, movement or role choice that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key artistic choices from the example and say what each one does for the audience or artwork.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful response names the element or technique and its effect, not only “it looks nice”.","explain":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final praise word without naming a choice does not show arts thinking. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Show the artistic choice","visual":[{"alt":"The decision to search the art room contrasted with choosing before the key is known to be missing.","caption":"The decision comes after the obstacle, not before the problem.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Decision</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">Search the art room</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Too early</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">Choose before the loss</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no artistic reason","Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about story beat?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nDeciding where to go before any obstacle leaves nothing to decide. A decision needs a reason. The closed office is that reason. Another mix-up is treating find as ''the key is back in the hand''. In this sequence, find means the loss is discovered. Put the beats back in order: loss, block, choice. Here is a more helpful example: The beats are the empty pocket, the closed office, and the choice to search. Find: Riley pats the zip pocket and shows Alex an empty hand. Obstacle: Alex points at the office shutter, which is already down. Decision: Riley says the bag was in the art room at lunch and turns that way. A swapped order shows why sequence matters. If they choose the art room before anyone has seen the empty pocket, the audience has no problem yet. The key itself is still not found. That recovery belongs to a later lesson.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against choice weakens the work, then write a stronger choice with a reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nDiscover the loss, meet the closed office, then choose the art-room search. The empty pocket is the find. The shutter is the obstacle. Turning toward the art room is the decision. Choosing the art room first would decide before the audience knows what is wrong. Finding the key in beat one would also skip the obstacle, and this lesson stops before the key returns.","explain":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the decoration without changing the artistic idea does not fix the error. Deciding where to go before any obstacle leaves nothing to decide. A decision needs a reason. The closed office is that reason. Another mix-up is treating find as ''the key is back in the hand''. In this sequence, find means the loss is discovered. Put the beats back in order: loss, block, choice. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no artistic reason","Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about story beat?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nA story beat is one action that pushes the key scene forward. This lesson adds sequence to the audience-knowledge list from last time. Three beats are enough: find, obstacle, decision. Here, find means discovering the key is missing, not getting it back. The obstacle is the problem that blocks an easy fix. The office shutter is down, so a replacement key is unavailable. The decision is the choice made because of that obstacle: Riley will search the art room, where the bag was at lunch. Order matters. The find creates the need, the obstacle removes the easy path, and the decision answers it. Write the three beats in that order. Performing them is optional, with consent and an adult nearby. Now choose a different subject or piece to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nMake or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your main choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should use the same arts idea as the model, but with different content or material.","explain":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to a new artwork or performance. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Transfer to a new artwork or performance"}],"title":"Year 7, Lesson 14: Playbuilding","unit_number":2,"unit_title":"Playbuilding","week":11,"week_in_unit":1,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nThe beats are the empty pocket, the closed office, and the choice to search. Find: Riley pats the zip pocket and shows Alex an empty hand. Obstacle: Alex points at the office shutter, which is already down. Decision: Riley says the bag was in the art room at lunch and turns that way. A swapped order shows why sequence matters. If they choose the art room before anyone has seen the empty pocket, the audience has no problem yet. The key itself is still not found. That recovery belongs to a later lesson.\n\nWrite what makes this example effective, naming the element, technique or performance choice and its effect.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"The find beat of an empty pocket beside the obstacle of a closed office shutter.","caption":"Find the loss first, then meet the obstacle.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Find</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">Empty pocket at the gate</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Obstacle</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">Office shutter is down</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"The decision to search the art room contrasted with choosing before the key is known to be missing.","caption":"The decision comes after the obstacle, not before the problem.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Decision</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">Search the art room</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Too early</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">Choose before the loss</text></g></svg>"}],"year_level":"Year 7"}') WHERE seed_key='s5-drama-y9-w11-l2' AND family_id IS NULL;
